#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cae0907-3675-5a01-9941-a578487ef6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398755} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8dc82f-4daf-5e3d-8677-ef427f30a4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398756} {:answer/id #uuid "f761bc1a-a386-5c89-8a33-3a4c6f055e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398757} {:answer/id #uuid "1d11de10-3f66-5ca8-8870-c7828e87e0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398758} {:answer/id #uuid "a82ad83c-fac7-5e3a-906c-294cfded72b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398759} :answer-field/id #uuid "523cfcb9-4d66-5dcd-b948-098533792d0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6610a46-90b9-5a9e-9cf1-d65ca11337a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332595" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}){e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89917edb-2315-5290-89a5-1760952c8a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398762} {:answer/id #uuid "fffd262c-0068-596e-9ac0-00fd88b6bcf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398763} {:answer/id #uuid "3910513d-9f11-530f-a539-554e1501e899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398764} {:answer/id #uuid "fcdc809a-8332-5157-8c0a-6ec6b0d41dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398765} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd6675b-5072-5356-ae13-83e65ece4812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398766}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398763} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9eb8731-2fb9-5537-93c3-a4e671793974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b74c0fe3-3f12-5992-93c1-45a8876eedf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332739" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution has the same exponential factor, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66613f28-b688-5af5-87c4-a12f46f9bd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399140} {:answer/id #uuid "b467a51e-f9be-5bed-89bc-7ff012c894de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399141} {:answer/id #uuid "149698b5-3361-5f52-a32c-6ed656acfe8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399142} {:answer/id #uuid "748a5bc0-46b6-5698-846f-66d6952f985d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399143} {:answer/id #uuid "99a4d6e3-caf9-544f-ba67-8f950954be82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399140} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2967834-56c7-54af-a045-1eb688f37565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8179a1bc-9dd2-5c9c-a7dd-3de3a7cd250c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80917" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eba8c50d-8cd6-567a-b472-75707ce03343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399147} {:answer/id #uuid "5e646bbf-5ae7-5023-a552-e22b215170d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399148} {:answer/id #uuid "6f01c0dd-442a-5b90-8b62-5974e56662f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399149} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d6bed6-49f3-574f-a78d-c14c6e9719ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399150} {:answer/id #uuid "645e0e17-2268-567a-ac17-75ba1595de6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399148} :answer-field/id #uuid "162ccab1-6783-5e36-870b-96a5c241f52b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1bd476b7-be13-5d1b-9c4d-aad2517551db" :question/math-academy-id "q-80919" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1+1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c610096-f546-51ed-933a-3503175a90be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399284} {:answer/id #uuid "0b54db3c-8d6d-526f-a175-03ba6ce2af5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399285} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0e9dfa-1e7d-5276-a512-39501b2a2767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399286} {:answer/id #uuid "88dcd79e-fa94-516e-9b4f-1e0158b64a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399287} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb12f7f-6eed-5940-a40a-4ec06ffa1f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399288} :answer-field/id #uuid "5009b981-acee-537f-a90c-d9fc4554b09c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "20513819-0985-5cdd-a8ca-960c927c6ced" :question/math-academy-id "q-95216" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 4$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6\\times 4}(R))=6\\cdot 4=24.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03772422-1c36-5538-ad98-2c341599f95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399561} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fca63d-f8ed-5f29-879b-652f96b8979f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399562} {:answer/id #uuid "d42309d2-7714-52af-b242-8be64341a7ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399563} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef8e9e4-6087-5646-8666-05b609a8e68f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399564} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7c55b5-02cc-530a-a0d0-c4f52cb2cc8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399565} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaa733b0-227a-5a23-8495-605d957fadaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7278f295-d94d-5655-a394-3d2aeae2f280" :question/math-academy-id "q-106847" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fed22c0d-0f9b-57d1-92a2-caecdc72420b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399568} {:answer/id #uuid "3dfb8aa6-6f92-55b7-84d4-1a32fd997449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399569} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6e2e5e-e175-5321-89f2-b8167e85c478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399570} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fa4db5-51d1-519e-a890-420dd4b64ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399571} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8948a3-a5b2-529f-a83b-2897c1197641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399572} :answer-field/id #uuid "35865859-bc92-5d3f-aac6-8f0761f92bd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4eefc328-7753-58ac-9913-74a31d74d60d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106849" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf06c8c-fa37-5fb4-9bae-97c3d61f33d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399575} {:answer/id #uuid "21c0bd19-0548-5d99-9e33-6c9f927b3b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399576} {:answer/id #uuid "703a2c37-9c3b-5152-85d4-282f6208240c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399577} {:answer/id #uuid "79134b3c-db1d-513d-a8c1-a59e34a636a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399578} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a10085-0e10-5d8b-b074-4aa9f88186b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399579} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2955c7f-5f50-5316-9dc4-5bd9b60c3d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1feecf50-2291-54b0-bdd0-e3e3488def82" :question/math-academy-id "q-106851" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "269a38f4-d50d-507d-a2fe-b6b9552ccaae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399715} {:answer/id #uuid "e06b713f-a822-5e21-a364-ba83171f6e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399716} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1fb2b4-2519-5080-98a6-8275a55a992e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399717} {:answer/id #uuid "c53343ca-8e57-512e-ba07-1f08dda987cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399718} {:answer/id #uuid "97ab7ea6-d839-514b-b71d-a9aadeb68fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399717} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3251d1-6964-5600-84fe-fbb97c423ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b32c3e49-95fd-5233-9aa7-ff2e20c39b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110754" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=1\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+1\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f726da6-7b87-558e-8b51-b7840bdc0abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399722} {:answer/id #uuid "dfad63f4-b83c-5439-a2d9-6fc118ca836e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399723} {:answer/id #uuid "30b8bb0c-f3dd-57f2-a3a9-f151350734f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399724} {:answer/id #uuid "421e6c1d-b7d5-5d36-a5a5-f3ccd5c75076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399725} {:answer/id #uuid "dbfb14db-bf18-5acc-8ed0-cb564150d8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399724} :answer-field/id #uuid "af12360f-3ae7-5782-b9f9-7e9d6d3e8888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4400c4d4-e4b9-52ce-9f78-148918755004" :question/math-academy-id "q-110756" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=1\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+1\\cdot {\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\color{black}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}}}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db32447-a4ce-5dff-9487-a82e227e41f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402711} {:answer/id #uuid "8275882b-84f3-5627-93d4-2c259ae73a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402712} {:answer/id #uuid "814b7b1e-69ce-596a-8267-686227a9ea9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402713} {:answer/id #uuid "4a769e20-30bc-5034-8119-ec017476bd69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402714} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbec318-84b4-5cc1-a7c5-50ee378933cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402711} :answer-field/id #uuid "17572c2b-682c-5ae4-a962-e180a05850b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4bfe7ea3-d20b-5a92-b7ac-e6c2ec81d721" :question/math-academy-id "q-40179" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}6x-4y \\\\ 2x-y \\\\ -5x+3y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vectors \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{i})\\ne 0$ for any $i$'s, we conclude that none of them belongs to the kernel of $\\,T.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "523c2229-b400-5c7a-8577-639c6b8af789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403803} {:answer/id #uuid "63eae5d2-78e3-5f74-b5e4-a5410101683d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403804} {:answer/id #uuid "40fafdc2-d2ba-5785-a820-7bc80cae9803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403805} {:answer/id #uuid "53c00d30-d1f5-5321-b731-b01cf5c82916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403806} {:answer/id #uuid "69e79a65-9bd9-5bb6-9162-647d1a1434fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403807} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9f95a1a-2498-5647-bcee-22f3ad54c88b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c7bd6831-c61b-5654-9e45-1822cb85cf38" :question/math-academy-id "q-75099" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53040db9-250a-56a5-aaac-6f9de1208aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403810} {:answer/id #uuid "38d13893-1722-5c2a-ae81-602f74f9eec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403811} {:answer/id #uuid "61f534ac-fe4c-590c-9a9b-8f99140fd7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403812} {:answer/id #uuid "94317c8c-0c37-5d0c-8721-a8b615d86c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403813} {:answer/id #uuid "be9b0408-7cf7-5c46-b6d4-9c65348a7946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403812} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08b2c1b-6e45-549d-8e75-f22a5e13ebd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3ec22232-a046-59e8-a23e-1122c9545c1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75100" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ef9d2af-2241-5be9-9fa4-a8e93a74b58c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403817} {:answer/id #uuid "40956895-fd15-5548-bb48-c3389b38029e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403818} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0c39e5-8073-5dcf-994d-9e5a575fc526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403819} {:answer/id #uuid "66df6fbd-34ea-5bc6-80a0-f363e4fe5e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403820} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0496f0-a286-58c3-82dc-168d307fbe1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403820} :answer-field/id #uuid "844b700b-f1e6-59c0-ad41-788e19bacce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "884fc6a3-6df8-5fa0-a711-f28bd28480fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-75102" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5009b51a-3311-5349-a0d4-9263093c408c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404179} {:answer/id #uuid "15868a01-ce63-5e6b-b375-fec48a6e6ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404180} {:answer/id #uuid "55de43a8-0397-50c5-867b-d34348940323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404181} {:answer/id #uuid "5c165536-c143-5156-8063-ecfdc1329b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404182} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1de765-7e1f-51a4-a847-e7a575a67eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404179} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e82b63e-4a76-52fe-b5a0-9529b5e4e1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8fe978ee-e242-5740-b3cc-178c387af89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81386" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+4y \\\\ y+z \\\\ x-4z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vectors \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the vectors that belong to the kernel of $T$ are ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047c19ef-c76a-57b3-b13e-81cac8438947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473112} {:answer/id #uuid "0f411e21-0bfc-5e60-be10-aa9385c60d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473113} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b5e6b2-6fa4-5699-91d3-98491ea01274" :answer-field/key "$U={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5e99f8-6d7b-5605-8eeb-768c689a1a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473114} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e8b7c2-03e8-52a8-8c30-1f1597b0a406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473114} :answer-field/id #uuid "2909a2f7-876c-5e10-a289-6837f83679fc" :answer-field/key "$V={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "829a1b56-ccd5-5381-aeca-6ba71098f21a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473116} {:answer/id #uuid "adcc7d05-7e81-525a-b650-a03adf317913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473117} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b28bdf-55b7-5470-b1f6-bd8182431d5f" :answer-field/key "$W={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9892c6c7-24de-57f2-a3e8-0b32de0c1349" :question/math-academy-id "q-40491" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})},\\,$ ${Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})},\\,$ and $\\,{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})}.$ \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=18-18=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=1-1=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaaf8ce1-2c2b-5bcc-925e-0f599acad60d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454172} {:answer/id #uuid "c647dd27-09cc-5bd3-810e-1d4746a2941e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454173} :answer-field/id #uuid "619d8289-a96c-58c5-9ea1-3220d6f4efec" :answer-field/key "$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454836} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aa5580c-81c0-5f4c-a754-31a21c559398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454174} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb76b41-f895-5d96-b445-577e31e07b34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454175} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa0354d6-0d92-52da-b49b-f1f1344babb1" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454837} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de933d44-9208-5817-bc6e-9512bd25a83a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454176} {:answer/id #uuid "5c945b34-ae4d-5fc2-b49a-c1b5910f20d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454176} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8df321-1ba8-5043-8b63-8be3125b1692" :answer-field/key "$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "455cce4b-aebd-5209-87a1-ec6ed81f2e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94867" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ 0 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69e86229-681b-5403-b631-b065124b1be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453234} {:answer/id #uuid "e78110f0-ca50-51aa-914f-e386bec6425a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453235} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cbdc373-c990-5dd3-8186-7aa74bd3bb9d" :answer-field/key "$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454367} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfdf83a2-6a63-5759-a053-db246c627b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453236} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9113d9-1b88-5970-a100-85c7aea2dbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453237} :answer-field/id #uuid "195daa73-27ff-5877-a458-d22d5ed1852b" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454368} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd8a2c9-e002-5140-8dcb-a07eb82acf68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453238} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c0ea91-328a-544b-b2c0-d0c461ef46cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453239} :answer-field/id #uuid "9796340d-a680-530e-86de-58d047300c0a" :answer-field/key "$\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b0d7b89-c3c8-5cee-b6a2-01b5cd1b1f67" :question/math-academy-id "q-107533" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}=a\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-b)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-c)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+d\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f998697-e815-585e-b0b5-3820b9ab9a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406958} {:answer/id #uuid "51054506-a98c-59d1-82db-eab8461f2cb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406959} {:answer/id #uuid "6c76efec-c230-59b8-bab5-b44500517e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406960} {:answer/id #uuid "80f4eee2-e47a-55c2-b5f0-e42eb70ff849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406962} {:answer/id #uuid "c3375fbb-f890-5cba-a4b4-7f53a73a78cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406960} :answer-field/id #uuid "20065c9e-4427-527a-a708-a46b3d2f2065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e92cb69b-a679-593b-8882-9cab8d26a8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124289" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v-1,\\,y=3v-2u-6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax+y+9,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v-1 \\\\ 3v-2u-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ -2u+3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot (\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}-\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\\\ y+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6) \\\\ 2\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3x+y+9 \\\\ 2x+y+8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=3x+y+9,\\,v=2x+y+8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+2+8=13.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e9827-741e-554d-b4bd-a580c12832ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} {:answer/id #uuid "637ff16c-2a36-5623-9258-1f3130b80075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407529} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8f4850-6613-5ef7-a355-f12825aba902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407532} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7dec3-53c9-52fb-9b61-30210ef0b89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407538} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ed5d0-4d06-561d-902d-1213f1013134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7eee37-4841-5d7e-83f1-ad47a007de82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69f0b9bd-9336-5c1c-91e3-bf72b33b1333" :question/math-academy-id "q-140847" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16877fa0-d3a3-5da8-ba77-c7e71244a7c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407521} {:answer/id #uuid "6d264661-257d-5c8a-8684-122b5c0a6807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} {:answer/id #uuid "9555f6b0-dd96-561e-b956-a28b10613e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407535} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0b779d-af50-58f8-97aa-dd75027108dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407536} {:answer/id #uuid "d6231cbd-de79-555a-b056-b8008595ed25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1092e23-b9a3-5556-b0d1-0745f95be4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e3f233e-913d-50cf-8912-1c6832afca12" :question/math-academy-id "q-95725" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-65 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f090535-e10a-5c59-9a51-2d2aae1cbc9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} {:answer/id #uuid "3f43beb7-1a8e-5d63-bf78-07894e8c2c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407527} {:answer/id #uuid "5613d7c8-bedb-54d2-be91-7e80b596eb5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407528} {:answer/id #uuid "694df5b3-0ed3-5486-8f0d-63bf6d2b9e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407530} {:answer/id #uuid "bc550768-52c7-5626-83cb-470760bd86c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49b921e-a620-50dc-993e-f6b1a37ca3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b502656e-ef35-5f6c-921c-ae3a8ea1f2a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95721" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-25 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32553198-4247-5c51-aaff-735cd1f400b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410240} {:answer/id #uuid "5089ccec-dbdc-5176-876d-be7464e512d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410258} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1835d4-2d7a-5203-af02-da60f52fa656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410305} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0dabcb-1994-53de-9ca9-1943be5e5cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410320} {:answer/id #uuid "af3ec0f0-e34b-503b-876e-317e6bdbb214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410320} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0fcff68-ef82-5cf2-80e9-66e229051d3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "903f2879-32c5-5f80-9994-a71eea52244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138637" :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143b59b0-133f-55a9-aba4-577093e492df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410522} {:answer/id #uuid "47588aca-363b-5cbe-b2d3-66854ec8696e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410523} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6e1323-5cbb-5d05-81d7-ed37a2420f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410524} {:answer/id #uuid "76ef7f6d-ef95-5749-93fb-20eeddd0b606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410526} {:answer/id #uuid "a84f8fed-839c-579a-aec7-743be12c797b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410530} :answer-field/id #uuid "f77cbe95-6bf7-5129-86d2-ca625369224e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1e15f70-9949-53c2-8d3b-1dd646d018c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79311" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}})=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74a173e3-4894-57a1-b114-4cebc35d441c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410525} {:answer/id #uuid "95748133-fe39-5ce6-8d35-f5c78273de56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410529} {:answer/id #uuid "b88d0552-0930-5b71-a537-c45316717b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410534} {:answer/id #uuid "dba05347-6a57-5e05-bc58-97062bc9c25c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410535} {:answer/id #uuid "f02020d2-3fed-5ef7-a4e4-c92e5db8fab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410535} :answer-field/id #uuid "620ffecc-6446-571f-9e30-35668717eb9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a83939de-1a77-573b-b113-0b677761ad51" :question/math-academy-id "q-35272" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}})=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dfffefc-7dc4-503a-9b1f-a84d08149c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413826} {:answer/id #uuid "672f41ac-64c5-58b5-868f-7c1ad340ab36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413827} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa05af9-3949-5d3f-84eb-7ccc92942416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413828} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7ac799-30a1-5efa-892d-6a6142f8760c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413829} {:answer/id #uuid "fb961936-d8ed-5956-ad00-aabdf66df6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413829} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eaed666-f3ff-5e76-bdcf-86e2bff8289c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9318e8aa-b34c-5ef4-bb58-533fb20cecad" :question/math-academy-id "q-80606" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2-1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{w}_{1}+9{w}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+3{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=3{w}_{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=3,b=-1,$ and $a+b=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf34227-df92-59d8-9613-ceb5484789cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413956} :answer-field/id #uuid "25dde8d8-265c-54c9-ac3f-e40a69e95898" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c37bfdb-2dfe-5cc2-840b-243db54502a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413959}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413959} :answer-field/id #uuid "506c651f-f5b6-52a6-9177-275eb76f9e87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be1c477-86a4-5471-a907-e291cdd9c2e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413957} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fabe0ba-2f06-5d33-ae09-be09b22d98ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0ade4ad-0a6f-502d-8256-a7ee48405353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413981} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4498fa0-0077-5245-b464-34ae73a87b7d" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413996} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb01895-358d-5093-891c-8725486935f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413960} :answer-field/id #uuid "e436c140-e683-55ba-81fe-1b3bbe91bf3f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c827e8bb-8488-5496-9b36-15035f1d576c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413980} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c61432-7357-5400-9143-63f15ef64c3f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7819c8fe-793f-5300-ba05-a00d3d72cc5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-253884" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{1}(3) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(0)\\end{bmatrix})} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(3),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(3)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(0),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(0)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(0)}{2} \\\\ 0 & f(0) & {f}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(0)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & {e}^{0} & \\frac{{e}^{0}}{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{0} & {e}^{0} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{0}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(0))} & ={e}^{({J}_{1}(3))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{3}(0))} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0cfcb58-9d82-5239-b7a2-55b6a5aec3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414514} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f30c989-de06-575e-b0bb-9f683d4312c4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414524} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c89c95f-fe9b-52f0-91e5-40f76e3bbf32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414507} :answer-field/id #uuid "72b4e588-1c2d-5b01-8c53-24f4c15496b7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414526} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b0faf5-c52f-5e9a-a0b2-40242658a8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414518}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414518} :answer-field/id #uuid "87ba22fc-b228-58c2-a360-feff99b2bf89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f0ab548-1239-59aa-8a8e-1144624f4ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-333011" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+2 \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  | $\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $2$ |  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1})-({a}_{2}{t}^{2}+{b}_{2}t+{c}_{2}) \\\\ {a}_{1}{t}^{2}+{b}_{1}t+{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{t}^{2}+4t-4 \\\\ -{t}^{2}-2t+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ 0{t}^{2}+2{a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-3){t}^{2}+(2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4)t+(2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}-4) \\\\ ({a}_{1}-1){t}^{2}+({b}_{1}-2)t+({c}_{1}+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-3 &=0 \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4 &=2{a}_{1} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}-4 &={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1}-1 &=0 \\\\ {b}_{1}-2 &=2{a}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+1 &={b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the fourth equation and then substituting the corresponding values ​​into the first and fifth equations to solve for ${a}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1,\\,{a}_{2}=-1,\\,{b}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe substitute these into the second equation to solve for ${b}_{2},$ and then into the sixth and third equations to solve for ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ We finally get\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=2,\\,{c}_{1}=-1,\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -\\,{t}^{2}+2t-2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a7977b-7339-57b7-a923-e8d0d8e4ad67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414500} {:answer/id #uuid "170117e4-3734-55f1-8a0e-dfa50d72211d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414501} {:answer/id #uuid "21d2e471-2f9a-5c9f-a4df-7096e53f0d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414503} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcbc6a3-2212-5cc2-8998-578a7cd88408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414504} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a09ee3-dc65-57fb-bc76-d9010cfcc444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414517}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414501} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a52ada-8c54-5615-81ca-fa8f0f0a0820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47fd4ff4-6ba7-5ef0-9848-b6ea043ad002" :question/math-academy-id "q-332740" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere (\\mathbf{x}_c(t)) is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution has the same exponential factor, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72cc1442-df79-5623-bff4-42eedddafdb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414732} {:answer/id #uuid "77e7d035-74cb-5a21-8a35-1b0d4898e245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414733} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa54ac6-f50b-5137-953c-f00902b1d420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414738} {:answer/id #uuid "be937ae3-0668-5fe5-9548-61b224487acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414741} {:answer/id #uuid "c38c74ea-a21b-5c0e-bb44-84641a158c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414732} :answer-field/id #uuid "969beab5-314b-57a6-99da-182f476a0e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0934e8ac-174d-5792-a7fc-4ab5bbca2aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-41971" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18fa685c-26d9-50c3-9545-5630e0f95fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414724} {:answer/id #uuid "194427ad-263d-5d8c-83c2-9e6a530a1d0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414725} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1253b4-be89-5b75-90d7-9bf16f52d17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414729} {:answer/id #uuid "a926e36b-6239-5512-942a-761db7b00ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414740} {:answer/id #uuid "f308a53e-6685-5d66-b2f6-d1b95a0af889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414729} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce877fc-f404-5538-a461-7601462b178c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "44c51145-6075-534b-b54c-46371542f3eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-40737" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d1356a-94ea-5209-96d5-9d9418453443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414730} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea97cd7-7fc3-5a3d-bc31-abc4a8a14911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414734} {:answer/id #uuid "7edf9628-a06c-547d-b79b-2b8c3b2e00b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414735} {:answer/id #uuid "a319ab1d-aad5-5d1b-8415-3377cf75bf86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414739} {:answer/id #uuid "d937650c-2bf8-5168-848d-23a4e1590bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414735} :answer-field/id #uuid "546f2176-ce65-54a2-a805-2b4a861a5018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ea317f2-12b0-537c-bfd8-c2c151d6c85b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86849" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=0,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f092c3d-8556-5f6a-af54-6cf54502714d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416311} {:answer/id #uuid "1a899cfc-2b1e-5341-b7ff-75e82ccc677e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416312} {:answer/id #uuid "ee402ce0-013b-55b8-989b-cc8ea6267cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416317} {:answer/id #uuid "eef9c29f-2ba3-5765-9533-90dd311f676f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416318} {:answer/id #uuid "facc80f1-1b35-55ea-977b-cfdffdd16d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416319}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416312} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba894365-a618-5f73-93d7-cf99f582f802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c0bd3b27-d4c9-5213-b407-098030561a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139473" :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^{2}+(6-1)^{2}+(4-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6042ceb6-39dd-5861-ab98-2ba38c0dfad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416313} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6a5f2f-76cf-57a5-b55b-09e192c66fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416314} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a4b7c1-b28b-59e9-b2e0-44d2e77e5df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416315} {:answer/id #uuid "db2fadc7-9954-5d2d-9567-580bac75c277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416316} {:answer/id #uuid "ff56f552-cb5d-52ad-a5a1-61418ef3f448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416316} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7e3155-6118-5747-b4e5-472254e85f83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d0d68cfe-73a4-5cae-a88d-ce4c5ac5ed06" :question/math-academy-id "q-139469" :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{90}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\hat{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(-3-(-1))^{2}+(-3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c81834-42ef-5852-b301-a6d3cbb3c8fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416327} {:answer/id #uuid "970d7f5e-257f-5c6c-a87e-441a641de9a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416338} {:answer/id #uuid "9f06a3b6-8e48-5d5d-b47c-2bf33ee89c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416339} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4520b1-1d35-57bb-b9ad-3e48af9ee8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416349} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6421c2-2bc0-520a-be99-675b8f795b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416327} :answer-field/id #uuid "a54a21cb-93a2-537e-bb2f-71cc7631af9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f199eb7-3af6-57bb-a681-cef6be591a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139476" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{8}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453ed560-2a6a-584b-b775-2ac6a73e45b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416330} {:answer/id #uuid "4e677ef6-4718-55df-b04f-5323f885a475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416332} {:answer/id #uuid "75d24566-becf-5714-8a17-ca7340070ab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416335} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e29b1a-ee23-527d-a4ce-26194d144439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416340} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f21d52-3d12-5f8b-8383-908db610799b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416343} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de09353-fb9c-544f-8c67-c4ef2c4abd36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "15ea3713-dc0a-53ca-b70c-c22a6b79a105" :question/math-academy-id "q-86853" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29430249-c511-5b3f-b606-e04e258a7382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416328} {:answer/id #uuid "66516586-cecd-5e63-b1f0-d497a546cd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416334} {:answer/id #uuid "b038fe34-a16a-5a55-bf98-fc3b8439343d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416342} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c0d55a-c5e9-50c2-8df7-9355a909c1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416346} {:answer/id #uuid "f15ad28f-93e2-5d8b-b5d3-7600366bd867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416328} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b507b6b-07ae-51ff-8ef0-2463cc379cda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d93f91c3-07ee-5843-9236-38543eb4a92a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139474" :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2-0)^{2}+(6-0)^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fef03fc-79ed-52dc-bd31-1e9cf734dd91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416329} {:answer/id #uuid "5170028a-ab43-580a-a8b5-29da72f1fcd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416333} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4a4aea-dd6d-5e0a-99e0-02a535cf0fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416336} {:answer/id #uuid "c52aa776-edc2-57f3-a183-a9201feae1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416345} {:answer/id #uuid "f37da0e2-81e8-576a-aa5e-b3594802c8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416333} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5f218f-38ba-531a-af9e-df5b58a66d01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6dcfad9-4fa8-567a-ab9a-f61e9f6b1a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-39856" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$  \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce3ee34-0bbc-5bf1-8519-a5c6f5ca4be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416368} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfe1fac-b11a-54dc-b55d-c32d6ffe73e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416370} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed59ef4-4b16-5d39-970e-9eb55064e9c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416378} {:answer/id #uuid "afc0693e-b8b8-5f98-a567-53b12025e699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416381} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2eead7-3610-52a9-8cf6-5173ec43049c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416370} :answer-field/id #uuid "9715d32b-de54-5e1d-b216-07bf1e7e3fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5979cff9-340a-5b78-84fd-5ca3a8c1f370" :question/math-academy-id "q-140374" :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & b & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}x+y+z \\\\ x+z \\\\ y-z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -1 & -3\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & -3 & -2 & -6\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 4 & 6\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-5,$ $b=3,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+3+2=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6e04b1-baff-5b92-8e15-ea0dfbc34cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416366} {:answer/id #uuid "6f10e2cb-3c02-510f-8245-4c4fef2fb57e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416376} {:answer/id #uuid "d919b397-4a17-5c1a-b41c-a4d8613b1b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416384} {:answer/id #uuid "e2593c00-382d-5cff-bc6a-79ef94821d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416386} {:answer/id #uuid "f8dab090-6ca4-5789-8aba-16cd40d5c9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416390} :answer-field/id #uuid "d40ebe53-c259-5fc7-9393-ddb15fac1751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e8f9b1b9-d1e3-5a9e-a4fd-34ae92b559e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-140360" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}6x-7y \\\\ -2x-6y\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}50 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}6 & 4 & 50 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 & -20\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 & -20\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{10}{3} & \\frac{50}{3} & -\\frac{50}{3}\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{10}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -5\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8df6e2-fe2b-50c4-8d6d-bfcfdcb03b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416955} :answer-field/id #uuid "68f52881-eb76-51b7-8183-8acacfe3a8e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416973} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6019430-865a-5e00-8e5e-9f4fec670079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416967} :answer-field/id #uuid "cde49d9c-ec17-5210-bdd7-9e30e90fa8b4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f56bf75-c034-5aab-87bb-3f506f067872" :question/math-academy-id "q-350412" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices above, write the following expression as a Hadamard product. Here $(\\,\\cdot \\,)$ denotes matrix multiplication.\n\n| $(AB)\\cdot (By)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $⊙$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-6$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBy=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AB)\\cdot (By) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{diag}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339eb9d7-c3ff-5722-b2a4-62c01e506dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417130} {:answer/id #uuid "7545326f-2ed3-5b45-b83f-fdce64ffd43b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417142} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ab3da6-d52a-52fe-8824-525245387581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417162} {:answer/id #uuid "f99a0a3e-8e69-5c5a-8d55-fb74464b4d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417171} {:answer/id #uuid "fa770ac4-af40-5a3c-b0e8-fb5ff74934d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417172} :answer-field/id #uuid "61a8f832-f2b3-5123-8233-870da3bb21e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15cdd9a6-5092-51ed-93a5-b2dd11d2ff12" :question/math-academy-id "q-82713" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dea2883-7aed-50ab-88db-338d6b05d1d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430953} {:answer/id #uuid "6d08ac3a-bf71-57e5-86bb-45513f396dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}6{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430958} {:answer/id #uuid "78246ec8-609c-551f-b0ac-0dadda339bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430960} {:answer/id #uuid "a3181278-1876-5206-9569-f7c580389091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430963} {:answer/id #uuid "bcdbf8dd-4179-511e-ac05-173b55d1fa27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430960} :answer-field/id #uuid "59767641-5ff1-522d-98ff-ca8331e026ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87edb91a-a643-5fdb-a1df-2e5d059d6b9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-111154" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have one free variable, ${x}_{2}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00506e35-c298-5a4f-af8f-eeaca76aaacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417839} {:answer/id #uuid "00f093fe-bc06-5628-b62a-e67a521941bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417841} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7a673d-e4a8-5264-a60e-b71b9cc758bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417853} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7fd9d7-1875-5e28-9d53-6079344fc1c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417857} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d3edfc-faf6-56f0-8a2f-d23798f45713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417853} :answer-field/id #uuid "389ac663-f6cc-5412-8d70-af0f32538d7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "169ca18b-315b-5001-b32b-2f9efd14b654" :question/math-academy-id "q-86755" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-60\\,}{\\,\\,\\,30\\,}b \\\\  & =-2b \\\\  & =-2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4730af-deb8-5685-8db4-054a0f26476b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418737} {:answer/id #uuid "45409e4f-d6f7-583a-a654-f6549650ec01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418740} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf16aae-72e1-5107-ae16-5b805fc64b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418742} {:answer/id #uuid "510cc82f-89b0-512e-a913-16359c5c9470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418743} {:answer/id #uuid "d7982ef7-967e-53d6-ac5f-6caebe695c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418743} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71e16f6-f24e-58ff-9688-d083942b3ca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "95765da4-bd97-53db-8498-26a824409464" :question/math-academy-id "q-80524" :question/problem "Given that $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{2+2}{1}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aa3f0cb-f552-5997-9604-53d87b356492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4z \\\\ y \\\\ 2z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455062} {:answer/id #uuid "967cf1df-e3fe-5921-8f01-b706d00a2a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 4x+2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,x,y\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455175} {:answer/id #uuid "b52464b5-12d3-55df-8a1f-c2ec33198abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,y,z\\in R\\}." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455221} {:answer/id #uuid "c0727ab1-37f4-52c5-b6e4-c5118fe93bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 4x+2y\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,x,y\\in R\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455234} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2608f3-a0b5-5f12-8da7-ae3d41d9398a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455221} :answer-field/id #uuid "07ed2ad4-a2fd-5d04-be73-f4b3ba6ae7db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "32b7b7cc-73cb-5678-9200-bd4242fb8265" :question/math-academy-id "q-140056" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x+4y-2z \\\\ -3x-6y+3z \\\\ 4x+8y-4z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nSetting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y+z.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,y,z\\in R\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6934d8-eaf0-5f13-b06a-71ac2a0dc0af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419660} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9c3643-ad71-51e0-bd19-40d272c2cabe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419667} {:answer/id #uuid "8f47a664-a9ad-5deb-90c8-17a7a38adab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 7 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419671} {:answer/id #uuid "d0704113-7386-54ca-ad0c-7578acd9ac62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -11 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419676} {:answer/id #uuid "dc81c6b4-cc09-56a5-916c-e323cc4197ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419667} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c807d8-09ff-597f-a633-55d26150ec43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "662e0d41-a6b8-58b3-ad4a-e835c48f17e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97118" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094350f2-e017-53e0-b0e9-5075268dbe4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419659} {:answer/id #uuid "248c47da-3dc5-5333-809d-acf62be00f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -12 \\\\ 7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419663} {:answer/id #uuid "7d527527-44f7-5758-b7fd-4c2cd30a0912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419670} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cdd80b-8123-585f-9ada-143e7f38d137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419674} {:answer/id #uuid "cd75b99f-ca84-5149-9b32-732c0d712c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & -18 \\\\ -9 & -16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419663} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3a3177-a079-5cdb-a624-7225c5f182a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7afd5a54-c223-5e70-9582-9406545b2d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-97279" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -12 \\\\ 7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ef60749-f0a3-5b92-b7ac-687d57c40bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423608} {:answer/id #uuid "842c1b53-7a3c-5c3c-95cd-f635e53b9e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423609} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1ff8c3-beef-5403-bf54-dedcfd80e6c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423610} {:answer/id #uuid "c1363c60-15a5-5bca-ae79-3b740d941738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423613} {:answer/id #uuid "c62c345c-75b2-5956-a3af-45a35e4a85f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423610} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d734853-a228-557f-9012-a5d01ef1cf52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c2bc939-4b4c-5161-9351-90818be5a0a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75070" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(V).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(V),$ we reduce the matrix $V$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a113c4d-a0ac-5651-906e-41d84f5d836e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424582} {:answer/id #uuid "5368a6fd-1c3c-5ec5-98c7-f3629d82df88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424583} {:answer/id #uuid "78554db1-b432-51b4-936b-3b8941909583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424585} {:answer/id #uuid "a841feda-cb6b-5b98-990a-d1a0abf6cfb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424588} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9dadc2-a499-5eb3-a904-a3da6ea8a443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424588} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd465c3-4977-5d35-b197-a5e9f9656a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4780154a-e6c5-5212-9585-41f4b2c7d38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34832" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c49f7c-a1f3-56e9-95d3-4e8d7959ede8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424584} {:answer/id #uuid "93ff253e-bb05-5ca6-8757-db5007d7fe23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424586} {:answer/id #uuid "9b73c0f1-2d21-5d2b-981e-9083bbda05f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424587} {:answer/id #uuid "bc38effb-2332-5570-8872-70a5b22fb92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424589} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcba230-afee-51b7-a5c4-70037d514241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424590}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424590} :answer-field/id #uuid "29131f28-d633-5715-a931-1a4a0aae454c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f427e4e2-13a5-5d7b-8b8d-103e6b378823" :question/math-academy-id "q-79320" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/ch